.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
This page details libusb's asynchronous (non-blocking) API for USB device I/O\&. 

This interface is very powerful but is also quite complex - you will need to read this page carefully to understand the necessary considerations and issues surrounding use of this interface\&. Simplistic applications may wish to consider the \fBsynchronous I/O API\fP instead\&.

.PP
The asynchronous interface is built around the idea of separating transfer submission and handling of transfer completion (the synchronous model combines both of these into one)\&. There may be a long delay between submission and completion, however the asynchronous submission function is non-blocking so will return control to your application during that potentially long delay\&.
.SH "Transfer abstraction"
.PP
For the asynchronous I/O, libusb implements the concept of a generic transfer entity for all types of I/O (control, bulk, interrupt, isochronous)\&. The generic transfer object must be treated slightly differently depending on which type of I/O you are performing with it\&.

.PP
This is represented by the public \fBlibusb_transfer\fP structure type\&.
.SH "Asynchronous transfers"
.PP
We can view asynchronous I/O as a 5 step process:
.IP "1." 4
\fBAllocation\fP: allocate a \fBlibusb_transfer\fP
.IP "2." 4
\fBFilling\fP: populate the \fBlibusb_transfer\fP instance with information about the transfer you wish to perform
.IP "3." 4
\fBSubmission\fP: ask libusb to submit the transfer
.IP "4." 4
\fBCompletion handling\fP: examine transfer results in the \fBlibusb_transfer\fP structure
.IP "5." 4
\fBDeallocation\fP: clean up resources
.PP
.SS "Allocation"
This step involves allocating memory for a USB transfer\&. This is the generic transfer object mentioned above\&. At this stage, the transfer is "blank" with no details about what type of I/O it will be used for\&.

.PP
Allocation is done with the \fBlibusb_alloc_transfer()\fP function\&. You must use this function rather than allocating your own transfers\&.
.SS "Filling"
This step is where you take a previously allocated transfer and fill it with information to determine the message type and direction, data buffer, callback function, etc\&.

.PP
You can either fill the required fields yourself or you can use the helper functions: \fBlibusb_fill_control_transfer()\fP, \fBlibusb_fill_bulk_transfer()\fP and \fBlibusb_fill_interrupt_transfer()\fP\&.
.SS "Submission"
When you have allocated a transfer and filled it, you can submit it using \fBlibusb_submit_transfer()\fP\&. This function returns immediately but can be regarded as firing off the I/O request in the background\&.
.SS "Completion handling"
After a transfer has been submitted, one of four things can happen to it:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
The transfer completes (i\&.e\&. some data was transferred)
.IP "\(bu" 2
The transfer has a timeout and the timeout expires before all data is transferred
.IP "\(bu" 2
The transfer fails due to an error
.IP "\(bu" 2
The transfer is cancelled
.PP

.PP
Each of these will cause the user-specified transfer callback function to be invoked\&. It is up to the callback function to determine which of the above actually happened and to act accordingly\&.

.PP
The user-specified callback is passed a pointer to the \fBlibusb_transfer\fP structure which was used to setup and submit the transfer\&. At completion time, libusb has populated this structure with results of the transfer: success or failure reason, number of bytes of data transferred, etc\&. See the \fBlibusb_transfer\fP structure documentation for more information\&.

.PP
\fBImportant Note\fP: The user-specified callback is called from an event handling context\&. It is therefore important that no calls are made into libusb that will attempt to perform any event handling\&. Examples of such functions are any listed in the \fBsynchronous API\fP and any of the blocking functions that retrieve \fBUSB descriptors\fP\&.
.SS "Deallocation"
When a transfer has completed (i\&.e\&. the callback function has been invoked), you are advised to free the transfer (unless you wish to resubmit it, see below)\&. Transfers are deallocated with \fBlibusb_free_transfer()\fP\&.

.PP
It is undefined behaviour to free a transfer which has not completed\&.
.SH "Resubmission"
.PP
You may be wondering why allocation, filling, and submission are all separated above where they could reasonably be combined into a single operation\&.

.PP
The reason for separation is to allow you to resubmit transfers without having to allocate new ones every time\&. This is especially useful for common situations dealing with interrupt endpoints - you allocate one transfer, fill and submit it, and when it returns with results you just resubmit it for the next interrupt\&.
.SH "Cancellation"
.PP
Another advantage of using the asynchronous interface is that you have the ability to cancel transfers which have not yet completed\&. This is done by calling the \fBlibusb_cancel_transfer()\fP function\&.

.PP
\fBlibusb_cancel_transfer()\fP is asynchronous/non-blocking in itself\&. When the cancellation actually completes, the transfer's callback function will be invoked, and the callback function should check the transfer status to determine that it was cancelled\&.

.PP
On macOS and iOS it is not possible to cancel a single transfer\&. In this case cancelling one transfer on an endpoint will cause all transfers on that endpoint to be cancelled\&.

.PP
Freeing the transfer after it has been cancelled but before cancellation has completed will result in undefined behaviour\&.

.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
When a transfer is cancelled, some of the data may have been transferred\&. libusb will communicate this to you in the transfer callback\&. \fBDo not assume that no data was transferred\&.\fP
.RE
.PP
.SH "Partial data transfer resulting from cancellation"
.PP
As noted above, some of the data may have been transferred at the time a transfer is cancelled\&. It is helpful to see how this is possible if you consider a bulk transfer to an endpoint with a packet size of 64 bytes\&. Supposing you submit a 512-byte transfer to this endpoint, the operating system will divide this transfer up into 8 separate 64-byte frames that the host controller will schedule for the device to transfer data\&. If this transfer is cancelled while the device is transferring data, a subset of these frames may be descheduled from the host controller before the device has the opportunity to finish transferring data to the host\&.

.PP
What your application should do with a partial data transfer is a policy decision; there is no single answer that satisfies the needs of every application\&. The data that was successfully transferred should be considered entirely valid, but your application must decide what to do with the remaining data that was not transferred\&. Some possible actions to take are:
.IP "\(bu" 2
Resubmit another transfer for the remaining data, possibly with a shorter timeout
.IP "\(bu" 2
Discard the partially transferred data and report an error
.PP
.SH "Timeouts"
.PP
When a transfer times out, libusb internally notes this and attempts to cancel the transfer\&. As noted in \fBabove\fP, it is possible that some of the data may actually have been transferred\&. Your application should \fBalways\fP check how much data was actually transferred once the transfer completes and act accordingly\&.
.SH "Overflows on device-to-host bulk/interrupt endpoints"
.PP
If your device does not have predictable transfer sizes (or it misbehaves), your application may submit a request for data on an IN endpoint which is smaller than the data that the device wishes to send\&. In some circumstances this will cause an overflow, which is a nasty condition to deal with\&. See the \fBPackets and overflows\fP page for discussion\&.
.SH "Considerations for control transfers"
.PP
The \fR\fBlibusb_transfer\fP\fP structure is generic and hence does not include specific fields for the control-specific setup packet structure\&.

.PP
In order to perform a control transfer, you must place the 8-byte setup packet at the start of the data buffer\&. To simplify this, you could cast the buffer pointer to type struct \fBlibusb_control_setup\fP, or you can use the helper function \fBlibusb_fill_control_setup()\fP\&.

.PP
The wLength field placed in the setup packet must be the length you would expect to be sent in the setup packet: the length of the payload that follows (or the expected maximum number of bytes to receive)\&. However, the length field of the \fBlibusb_transfer\fP object must be the length of the data buffer - i\&.e\&. it should be wLength \fIplus\fP the size of the setup packet (LIBUSB_CONTROL_SETUP_SIZE)\&.

.PP
If you use the helper functions, this is simplified for you:
.IP "1." 4
Allocate a buffer of size LIBUSB_CONTROL_SETUP_SIZE plus the size of the data you are sending/requesting\&.
.IP "2." 4
Call \fBlibusb_fill_control_setup()\fP on the data buffer, using the transfer request size as the wLength value (i\&.e\&. do not include the extra space you allocated for the control setup)\&.
.IP "3." 4
If this is a host-to-device transfer, place the data to be transferred in the data buffer, starting at offset LIBUSB_CONTROL_SETUP_SIZE\&.
.IP "4." 4
Call \fBlibusb_fill_control_transfer()\fP to associate the data buffer with the transfer (and to set the remaining details such as callback and timeout)\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
Note that there is no parameter to set the length field of the transfer\&. The length is automatically inferred from the wLength field of the setup packet\&.
.PP

.IP "5." 4
Submit the transfer\&.
.PP

.PP
The multi-byte control setup fields (wValue, wIndex and wLength) must be given in little-endian byte order (the endianness of the USB bus)\&. Endianness conversion is transparently handled by \fBlibusb_fill_control_setup()\fP which is documented to accept host-endian values\&.

.PP
Further considerations are needed when handling transfer completion in your callback function:
.IP "\(bu" 2
As you might expect, the setup packet will still be sitting at the start of the data buffer\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
If this was a device-to-host transfer, the received data will be sitting at offset LIBUSB_CONTROL_SETUP_SIZE into the buffer\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
The actual_length field of the transfer structure is relative to the wLength of the setup packet, rather than the size of the data buffer\&. So, if your wLength was 4, your transfer's \fRlength\fP was 12, then you should expect an \fRactual_length\fP of 4 to indicate that the data was transferred in entirety\&.
.PP

.PP
To simplify parsing of setup packets and obtaining the data from the correct offset, you may wish to use the \fBlibusb_control_transfer_get_data()\fP and \fBlibusb_control_transfer_get_setup()\fP functions within your transfer callback\&.

.PP
Even though control endpoints do not halt, a completed control transfer may have a LIBUSB_TRANSFER_STALL status code\&. This indicates the control request was not supported\&.
.SH "Considerations for interrupt transfers"
.PP
All interrupt transfers are performed using the polling interval presented by the bInterval value of the endpoint descriptor\&.
.SH "Considerations for isochronous transfers"
.PP
Isochronous transfers are more complicated than transfers to non-isochronous endpoints\&.

.PP
To perform I/O to an isochronous endpoint, allocate the transfer by calling \fBlibusb_alloc_transfer()\fP with an appropriate number of isochronous packets\&.

.PP
During filling, set \fBtype\fP to \fBLIBUSB_TRANSFER_TYPE_ISOCHRONOUS\fP, and set \fBnum_iso_packets\fP to a value less than or equal to the number of packets you requested during allocation\&. \fBlibusb_alloc_transfer()\fP does not set either of these fields for you, given that you might not even use the transfer on an isochronous endpoint\&.

.PP
Next, populate the length field for the first num_iso_packets entries in the \fBiso_packet_desc\fP array\&. Section 5\&.6\&.3 of the USB2 specifications describe how the maximum isochronous packet length is determined by the wMaxPacketSize field in the endpoint descriptor\&. Two functions can help you here:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBlibusb_get_max_iso_packet_size()\fP is an easy way to determine the max packet size for an isochronous endpoint\&. Note that the maximum packet size is actually the maximum number of bytes that can be transmitted in a single microframe, therefore this function multiplies the maximum number of bytes per transaction by the number of transaction opportunities per microframe\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBlibusb_set_iso_packet_lengths()\fP assigns the same length to all packets within a transfer, which is usually what you want\&.
.PP

.PP
For outgoing transfers, you'll obviously fill the buffer and populate the packet descriptors in hope that all the data gets transferred\&. For incoming transfers, you must ensure the buffer has sufficient capacity for the situation where all packets transfer the full amount of requested data\&.

.PP
Completion handling requires some extra consideration\&. The \fBactual_length\fP field of the transfer is meaningless and should not be examined; instead you must refer to the \fBactual_length\fP field of each individual packet\&.

.PP
The \fBstatus\fP field of the transfer is also a little misleading:
.IP "\(bu" 2
If the packets were submitted and the isochronous data microframes completed normally, status will have value \fBLIBUSB_TRANSFER_COMPLETED\fP\&. Note that bus errors and software-incurred delays are not counted as transfer errors; the transfer\&.status field may indicate COMPLETED even if some or all of the packets failed\&. Refer to the \fBstatus\fP field of each individual packet to determine packet failures\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
The status field will have value \fBLIBUSB_TRANSFER_ERROR\fP only when serious errors were encountered\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Other transfer status codes occur with normal behaviour\&.
.PP

.PP
The data for each packet will be found at an offset into the buffer that can be calculated as if each prior packet completed in full\&. The \fBlibusb_get_iso_packet_buffer()\fP and \fBlibusb_get_iso_packet_buffer_simple()\fP functions may help you here\&.
.SH "Transfer length limitations"
.PP
Some operating systems may impose limits on the length of the transfer data buffer or, in the case of isochronous transfers, the length of individual isochronous packets\&. Such limits can be difficult for libusb to detect, so in most cases the library will simply try and submit the transfer as set up by you\&. If the transfer fails to submit because it is too large, \fBlibusb_submit_transfer()\fP will return \fBLIBUSB_ERROR_INVALID_PARAM\fP\&.

.PP
The following are known limits for control transfer lengths\&. Note that this length includes the 8-byte setup packet\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Linux (4,096 bytes)
.IP "\(bu" 2
Windows (4,096 bytes)
.PP
.SH "Memory caveats"
.PP
In most circumstances, it is not safe to use stack memory for transfer buffers\&. This is because the function that fired off the asynchronous transfer may return before libusb has finished using the buffer, and when the function returns it's stack gets destroyed\&. This is true for both host-to-device and device-to-host transfers\&.

.PP
The only case in which it is safe to use stack memory is where you can guarantee that the function owning the stack space for the buffer does not return until after the transfer's callback function has completed\&. In every other case, you need to use heap memory instead\&.
.SH "Fine control"
.PP
Through using this asynchronous interface, you may find yourself repeating a few simple operations many times\&. You can apply a bitwise OR of certain flags to a transfer to simplify certain things:
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBLIBUSB_TRANSFER_SHORT_NOT_OK\fP results in transfers which transferred less than the requested amount of data being marked with status \fBLIBUSB_TRANSFER_ERROR\fP (they would normally be regarded as COMPLETED)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBLIBUSB_TRANSFER_FREE_BUFFER\fP allows you to ask libusb to free the transfer buffer when freeing the transfer\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBLIBUSB_TRANSFER_FREE_TRANSFER\fP causes libusb to automatically free the transfer after the transfer callback returns\&.
.PP
.SH "Event handling"
.PP
An asynchronous model requires that libusb perform work at various points in time - namely processing the results of previously-submitted transfers and invoking the user-supplied callback function\&.

.PP
This gives rise to the \fBlibusb_handle_events()\fP function which your application must call into when libusb has work do to\&. This gives libusb the opportunity to reap pending transfers, invoke callbacks, etc\&.

.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
All event handling is performed by whichever thread calls the \fBlibusb_handle_events()\fP function\&. libusb does not invoke any callbacks outside of this context\&. Consequently, any callbacks will be run on the thread that calls the \fBlibusb_handle_events()\fP function\&.
.RE
.PP
When to call the \fBlibusb_handle_events()\fP function depends on which model your application decides to use\&. The 2 different approaches:

.PP
.IP "1." 4
Repeatedly call \fBlibusb_handle_events()\fP in blocking mode from a dedicated thread\&.
.IP "2." 4
Integrate libusb with your application's main event loop\&. libusb exposes a set of file descriptors which allow you to do this\&.
.PP

.PP
The first approach has the big advantage that it will also work on Windows were libusb' poll API for select / poll integration is not available\&. So if you want to support Windows and use the async API, you must use this approach, see the \fBUsing an event handling thread\fP section below for details\&.

.PP
If you prefer a single threaded approach with a single central event loop, see the \fBpolling and timing\fP section for how to integrate libusb into your application's main event loop\&.
.SH "Using an event handling thread"
.PP
Lets begin with stating the obvious: If you're going to use a separate thread for libusb event handling, your callback functions MUST be thread-safe\&.

.PP
Other then that doing event handling from a separate thread, is mostly simple\&. You can use an event thread function as follows: 
.PP
.nf
void *event_thread_func(void *ctx)
{
    while (event_thread_run)
        libusb_handle_events(ctx);

    return NULL;
}

.fi
.PP

.PP
There is one caveat though, stopping this thread requires setting the event_thread_run variable to 0, and after that \fBlibusb_handle_events()\fP needs to return control to event_thread_func\&. But unless some event happens, \fBlibusb_handle_events()\fP will not return\&.

.PP
There are 2 different ways of dealing with this, depending on if your application uses libusb' \fBhotplug\fP support or not\&.

.PP
Applications which do not use hotplug support, should not start the event thread until after their first call to \fBlibusb_open()\fP, and should stop the thread when closing the last open device as follows: 
.PP
.nf
void my_close_handle(libusb_device_handle *dev_handle)
{
    if (open_devs == 1)
        event_thread_run = 0;

    libusb_close(dev_handle); // This wakes up libusb_handle_events()

    if (open_devs == 1)
        pthread_join(event_thread);

    open_devs\-\-;
}

.fi
.PP

.PP
Applications using hotplug support should start the thread at program init, after having successfully called \fBlibusb_hotplug_register_callback()\fP, and should stop the thread at program exit as follows: 
.PP
.nf
void my_libusb_exit(void)
{
    event_thread_run = 0;
    libusb_hotplug_deregister_callback(ctx, hotplug_cb_handle); // This wakes up libusb_handle_events()
    pthread_join(event_thread);
    libusb_exit(ctx);
}

.fi
.PP
 
