\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
\fBLIBUSB_SUCCESS\fP, or a LIBUSB_ERROR code on failure 
.RE
.PP

.SS "struct \fBlibusb_transfer\fP * libusb_alloc_transfer (int iso_packets)"

.PP
Allocate a libusb transfer with a specified number of isochronous packet descriptors\&. The returned transfer is pre-initialized for you\&. When the new transfer is no longer needed, it should be freed with \fBlibusb_free_transfer()\fP\&.

.PP
Transfers intended for non-isochronous endpoints (e\&.g\&. control, bulk, interrupt) should specify an iso_packets count of zero\&.

.PP
For transfers intended for isochronous endpoints, specify an appropriate number of packet descriptors to be allocated as part of the transfer\&. The returned transfer is not specially initialized for isochronous I/O; you are still required to set the \fBnum_iso_packets\fP and \fBtype\fP fields accordingly\&.

.PP
It is safe to allocate a transfer with some isochronous packets and then use it on a non-isochronous endpoint\&. If you do this, ensure that at time of submission, num_iso_packets is 0 and that type is set appropriately\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIiso_packets\fP number of isochronous packet descriptors to allocate\&. Must be non-negative\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
a newly allocated transfer, or NULL on error 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void libusb_free_transfer (struct \fBlibusb_transfer\fP * transfer)"

.PP
Free a transfer structure\&. This should be called for all transfers allocated with \fBlibusb_alloc_transfer()\fP\&.

.PP
If the \fBLIBUSB_TRANSFER_FREE_BUFFER\fP flag is set and the transfer buffer is non-NULL, this function will also free the transfer buffer using the standard system memory allocator (e\&.g\&. free())\&.

.PP
It is legal to call this function with a NULL transfer\&. In this case, the function will simply return safely\&.

.PP
It is not legal to free an active transfer (one which has been submitted and has not yet completed)\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItransfer\fP the transfer to free 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int libusb_submit_transfer (struct \fBlibusb_transfer\fP * transfer)"

.PP
Submit a transfer\&. This function will fire off the USB transfer and then return immediately\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItransfer\fP the transfer to submit 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
0 on success 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_NO_DEVICE\fP if the device has been disconnected 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_BUSY\fP if the transfer has already been submitted\&. 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED\fP if the transfer flags are not supported by the operating system\&. 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_INVALID_PARAM\fP if the transfer size is larger than the operating system and/or hardware can support (see \fBTransfer length limitations\fP) 

.PP
another LIBUSB_ERROR code on other failure 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int libusb_cancel_transfer (struct \fBlibusb_transfer\fP * transfer)"

.PP
Asynchronously cancel a previously submitted transfer\&. This function returns immediately, but this does not indicate cancellation is complete\&. Your callback function will be invoked at some later time with a transfer status of \fBLIBUSB_TRANSFER_CANCELLED\&.\fP

.PP
This function behaves differently on Darwin-based systems (macOS and iOS):

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Calling this function for one transfer will cause all transfers on the same endpoint to be cancelled\&. Your callback function will be invoked with a transfer status of \fBLIBUSB_TRANSFER_CANCELLED\fP for each transfer that was cancelled\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
When built for macOS versions prior to 10\&.5, this function sends a \fRClearFeature(ENDPOINT_HALT)\fP request for the transfer's endpoint\&. (Prior to libusb 1\&.0\&.27, this request was sent on all Darwin systems\&.) If the device does not handle this request correctly, the data toggle bits for the endpoint can be left out of sync between host and device, which can have unpredictable results when the next data is sent on the endpoint, including data being silently lost\&. A call to \fBlibusb_clear_halt\fP will not resolve this situation, since that function uses the same request\&. Therefore, if your program runs on macOS < 10\&.5 (or libusb < 1\&.0\&.27), and uses a device that does not correctly implement \fRClearFeature(ENDPOINT_HALT)\fP requests, it may only be safe to cancel transfers when followed by a device reset using \fBlibusb_reset_device\fP\&.
.PP

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItransfer\fP the transfer to cancel 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
0 on success 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_NOT_FOUND\fP if the transfer is not in progress, already complete, or already cancelled\&. 

.PP
a LIBUSB_ERROR code on failure 
.RE
.PP

